{"question": "A 65-year old man presents with gradually worsening rigidity of his arms and legs and slowness in performing tasks. He says he has also noticed hand tremors, which increase at rest and decrease with focused movements. On examination, the patient does not swing his arms while walking and has a shortened, shuffling gait. An antiviral drug is prescribed which alleviates the patient\u2019s symptoms. Which of the following drugs was most likely prescribed to this patient?", "options": {"A": "Amantadine", "B": "Ribavirin", "C": "Acyclovir", "D": "Levodopa", "E": "Zidovudine"}, "pred_answer": "D", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Neurology", "Movement Disorders", "Pharmacology", "Geriatrics", "Psychiatry"], "option_domains": ["Neurology", "Pharmacology"], "question_analyses": {"Neurology": "The medical scenario described presents a patient with symptoms that are consistent with Parkinson's disease, a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra region of the brain. The symptoms described\u2014gradually worsening rigidity of the arms and legs, slowness in performing tasks (bradykinesia), and hand tremors that increase at rest and decrease with focused movements\u2014are all classic signs of Parkinson's disease. The patient's symptoms, particularly the tremors that are more pronounced at rest and diminish with movement, are known as resting tremor. This is a hallmark feature of Parkinson's disease. The rigidity and bradykinesia are also typical symptoms, and the patient's gait changes, such as the inability to swing his arms while walking and a shortened, shuffling gait, are further supportive of the diagnosis. The fact that an antiviral drug was prescribed and alleviated the patient\u2019s symptoms is noteworthy. Parkinson's disease is not caused by a viral infection, so the antiviral drug was likely prescribed for another reason. This could be due to a misdiagnosis or a concomi